# 🌐 Website Q&A AI Agent

A Streamlit web app that scrapes a website (or PDF) and lets you ask natural-language
questions about its content — in **English**, **Urdu**, or **Roman Urdu** — powered by
Groq's `llama-3.3-70b-versatile` model. Answers are grounded strictly in the scraped
content, with hallucination explicitly forbidden by the system prompt.

## ✨ Features

- 🔗 Paste any website URL — scraped with `trafilatura` (falls back to `BeautifulSoup`
  if extraction is too thin)
- 📄 Paste a direct PDF link — text extracted with `pdfplumber`
- 💬 Multi-turn chat with full conversation history sent to the LLM
- 🧠 Answers are grounded *only* in scraped content — the model is instructed to say
  "not mentioned on the page" instead of guessing
- 📝 Structured summaries on request (main topic, purpose, key points, calls-to-action)
- 🌍 Auto language matching (English / Urdu / Roman Urdu) + auto-detection of the
  scraped page's language
- 🆚 "Compare two websites" mode
- 🔍 Collapsible "Source excerpt" under each answer for transparency
- 📊 Word/character count of scraped content
- 🔄 One-click "Clear chat / New website" reset
- 🛡️ Friendly error handling everywhere — no raw tracebacks shown to users

## 🏗️ Project structure

```
website-qa-agent/
├── app.py              # Main Streamlit app (UI, session state, chat loop)
├── scraper.py          # HTML + PDF scraping, language detection
├── llm.py              # Groq API calls, system prompt, source-excerpt finder
├── requirements.txt
├── .env.example
├── .gitignore
└── README.md
```

## 🚀 Getting started (local)

1. **Clone and enter the project**
   ```bash
   git clone <your-repo-url>
   cd website-qa-agent
   ```

2. **Create a virtual environment (recommended)**
   ```bash
   python -m venv venv
   source venv/bin/activate      # Windows: venv\Scripts\activate
   ```

3. **Install dependencies**
   ```bash
   pip install -r requirements.txt
   ```

4. **Set up your API key**
   ```bash
   cp .env.example .env
   ```
   Then edit `.env` and paste your Groq API key (get one free at
   [console.groq.com/keys](https://console.groq.com/keys)):
   ```
   GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_your_real_key_here
   ```

5. **Run the app**
   ```bash
   streamlit run app.py
   ```
   The app will open at `http://localhost:8501`.

## ☁️ Deploying to Streamlit Community Cloud

1. Push this project to a GitHub repository.
2. Go to [share.streamlit.io](https://share.streamlit.io) and create a new app
   pointing at your repo, with `app.py` as the entry point.
3. In your app's **Settings → Secrets**, add:
   ```toml
   GROQ_API_KEY = "gsk_your_real_key_here"
   ```
4. Deploy. The app automatically reads from `st.secrets` on Streamlit Cloud and
   from `.env` locally — no code changes needed.

## 🧪 How it works

1. **Scraping** (`scraper.py`): the URL is checked for a `.pdf` extension or PDF
   content-type. HTML pages are fetched with a browser-like `User-Agent` to avoid
   403s, then parsed first with `trafilatura`; if that returns too little text, it
   falls back to a `BeautifulSoup` pass over `<h1>`–`<h3>`, `<p>`, and `<li>` tags.
2. **Caching**: the scraped text is stored in `st.session_state` and reused for
   every question — the page is only scraped once, when you click "Scrape & Start".
3. **Chat**: each question, plus full conversation history, is sent to Groq
   alongside a system prompt containing the scraped content and strict grounding
   rules (see `llm.py::build_system_prompt`).
4. **Source excerpts**: a lightweight keyword-overlap heuristic
   (`llm.py::find_relevant_excerpt`) highlights the sentences in the scraped
   content most related to your question, shown in a collapsible section under
   each answer — a hint about the answer's basis, not a formal citation.

## 🖥️ Handling JavaScript-heavy websites (optional)

Some sites (React/Next.js/Vue single-page apps) load their real content via
client-side JavaScript, so a plain HTTP scraper only sees an empty page shell.
This app already tries several free fallbacks first — parsing structured
`<script type="application/ld+json">` data and embedded framework state
(e.g. Next.js `__NEXT_DATA__`) — which resolves many cases without any extra
setup. If a page is still too thin after that, you'll see a warning in the
app suggesting you install Playwright for a real headless-browser render:

```bash
pip install playwright
playwright install chromium
```

Then just restart the app — no code changes needed. This is optional; the
app works without it, just with reduced accuracy on heavily JS-rendered pages.

## 🔁 If you get a "model decommissioned" or "AI response" error

Groq periodically retires older models. If chat requests start failing, check
[console.groq.com/docs/deprecations](https://console.groq.com/docs/deprecations)
for the current recommended model, then update `MODEL_NAME` at the top of `llm.py`.
As of this project's last update, the model in use is `openai/gpt-oss-120b`
(Groq's recommended replacement for the now-retired `llama-3.3-70b-versatile`).

## ⚠️ Limitations

- JavaScript-heavy sites that render content client-side may not scrape well
  (no headless browser is used).
- Scanned/image-only PDFs won't yield text (no OCR is included).
- The "source excerpt" feature uses simple keyword overlap, not true semantic
  retrieval — treat it as a helpful pointer, not a guarantee.
- Language detection (`langdetect`) is probabilistic and can be wrong on short text.

